# What do sensitivity and specificity measure?
subtitle: "Why a test that is 90% sensitive and 95% specific can still be wrong about most positives when the disease is rare."
voices: {"a":"gemini:Iapetus | a clear, patient medical-school teacher; plain and steady","take":"lines"}

## What do sensitivity and specificity measure?
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Test result
    col_label: Really has the disease?
    row_values:
      - Positive
      - Negative
    col_values:
      - ''
      - ''
    cells:
      - [' ', ' ']
      - [' ', ' ']
sources:
    - id: yerushalmy1947
      title: Statistical problems in assessing methods of medical diagnosis, with special reference to X-ray techniques
      authors: Yerushalmy J
      year: 1947
      finding: Introduced sensitivity and specificity as measures of a diagnostic method, in readings of chest X-rays (Public Health Reports 62:1432–49).

    math v00 tex "180" size 34 x 385 y 448 hidden true
    math w00 tex "18" size 34 x 385 y 448 hidden true
    text n00 "true positive" #5a534c x 385 y 400 font_size 20 hidden true
    math v01 tex "40" size 34 x 615 y 448 hidden true
    math w01 tex "49" size 34 x 615 y 448 hidden true
    text n01 "false positive" #5a534c x 615 y 400 font_size 20 hidden true
    math v10 tex "20" size 34 x 385 y 298 hidden true
    math w10 tex "2" size 34 x 385 y 298 hidden true
    text n10 "false negative" #5a534c x 385 y 250 font_size 20 hidden true
    math v11 tex "760" size 34 x 615 y 298 hidden true
    math w11 tex "931" size 34 x 615 y 298 hidden true
    text n11 "true negative" #5a534c x 615 y 250 font_size 20 hidden true
    math h0 tex "\\text{Yes: } 200" size 28 x 385 y 550 hidden true
    math h1 tex "\\text{No: } 800" size 28 x 615 y 550 hidden true
    text N "1000 tested" #5a534c x 150 y 565 font_size 24 hidden true
    math sens2 tex "\\frac{18}{20} = 90\\%" x 385 y 172 hidden true
    math spec2 tex "\\frac{931}{980} = 95\\%" x 615 y 172 hidden true
    math ppv2 tex "\\frac{18}{67} = 27\\%" x 865 y 435 hidden true

    card title "What do sensitivity and specificity measure?"

What do sensitivity and specificity actually tell you about a test? Test a thousand people, and sort them two ways.
    draw grid row_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_title h0 h1 N

Two hundred of them really have the disease. The test catches 180: true positives.
    draw cell_0_0 v00 n00 parallel true

But it misses twenty: false negatives, sick people sent home reassured.
    draw cell_1_0 v10 n10 parallel true

Of the 800 healthy people, 760 correctly test negative.
    draw cell_1_1 v11 n11 parallel true

And forty test positive anyway: false positives, healthy people told they may be ill.
    draw cell_0_1 v01 n01 parallel true

    pause 0.4

Sensitivity reads down the sick column: of those who have the disease, how many does the test catch?
    highlight h0 v00 v10

180 of 200: 90 percent.
    math sens tex "\\frac{180}{200} = 90\\%" x 385 y 172
    text l_sens "sensitivity" left sens gap 14 #b5482e cites ["yerushalmy1947"]

Specificity reads down the healthy column: of those without it, how many does it clear?
    highlight h1 v01 v11

760 of 800: 95 percent.
    math spec tex "\\frac{760}{800} = 95\\%" x 615 y 172
    text l_spec "specificity" right spec gap 14 #2f6b8f

Both are counted among people whose true state we already know. So they describe the test itself.
    highlight h0 h1

But a patient holding a positive result asks another question, reading across the row.
    highlight row_header_0 v00 v01

Of 220 positives, 180 are ill: 82 percent.
    math ppv tex "\\frac{180}{220} = 82\\%" x 865 y 435
    label l_ppv "positive and ill" above #8a5fa8 attach_to ppv

    erase v00 v01 v10 v11 sens spec ppv parallel true

Now use the same test where only 20 in a thousand are ill.
    morph h0 tex "\\text{Yes: } 20"

It still catches 18 of them, and misses 2.
    draw w00 w10 parallel true

    morph h1 tex "\\text{No: } 980"

But 49 of the 980 healthy people test positive too.
    draw w11 w01 parallel true

So the row turns: of 67 positives, only 18 are ill. 27 percent.
    draw ppv2

Yet sensitivity has not moved: 18 of 20, still 90 percent.
    draw sens2

Nor has specificity. The columns belong to the test; the row depends on how common the disease is.
    draw spec2

Jacob Yerushalmy gave the two terms their meaning in 1947, comparing how well doctors read chest X-rays for tuberculosis.
    highlight l_sens l_spec

    quiz "The same test is used in a clinic where half the patients have the disease. What is its sensitivity?"
        right: Still 90 percent: sensitivity is counted only among the sick, so it does not depend on how many are sick. What changes is how often a positive is right.
        + Still 90%
        * Higher than 90%
        * Lower than 90%

## Thumbnail
page: {"valign":"none"}
role: thumbnail

    icon test size 300 set twemoji x 230 y 470 icon_look picture of "test tube"
    text a "90 % right" #2f6b8f x 680 y 590 font_size 80
    text c "most positives wrong?" #b5482e x 640 y 420 font_size 60
    thumb w1 "Good test, bad news?" kind band
